2026-11-28 05:21:09,232 - INFO - [MainThread] - Received claude code message: {'content': [{'tool_use_id': 'call-6bfb2c7a60624da3a512a205b80af9bb', 'content': '1\tGiven the reference image, reconstruct the depicted scene as an editable 3D scene in Blender.\n2\t\n3\tYou may write and execute Blender Python code, inspect the generated scene, render images, and revise your implementation.\n4\t\n5\tYour objective is to reconstruct the reference scene as faithfully as possible, including visible objects, geometry, spatial relationships, materials, lighting, and camera viewpoint.\n6\t\n7\tChoose your own reconstruction strategy and workflow.\n8\t\n9\tUse the provided reference image and the available Blender environment. Do not retrieve an existing reconstruction or access external reference scene data.\n10\t\n11\tDeliver:\n12\t\n13\t- Executable Blender Python source code.\n14\t- The reconstructed .blend scene.\n15\t- A final rendered image.\n16\t\n17\tWorkspace and resources:\n18\tYour writable task workspace is /app. The reference image is also available at /app/reference.png. Blender 5.2.2 is available as blender. Rendering uses CPU only with eight threads. The student time budget is 25 minutes, with at most 120 model requests. Save the executable source entry point as /app/build.py, the editable final scene as /app/scene.blend, and the final rendered image as /app/final.png at 640 x 360 pixels. Keep any other required local source or material resources in /app so the delivered source can reproduce the scene.\n19\t\n20\tFrozen general capability knowledge:\n21\t# Editable reconstruction capability library\n22\t\n23\tUse a single RGB as a set of constraints, not a ground-truth 3-D blueprint. Hidden geometry, absolute scale, light/material decomposition and camera/layout are underdetermined. Block the room and large objects first; use a consistent arbitrary scale and distinguish observation from hypotheses. Inspect major silhouettes, semantic facing, occlusion, vanishing directions, aspect ratios and object gaps before decorative details. Iterate camera and layout together: a plausible camera can make an incorrect layout look right, and vice versa. Preserve variants and compare fixed-camera renders before changing the view. No independent transfer improvement has been established.\n24\t\n25\tThe Python module is imported inside Blender (add this directory to `sys.path`). No function renders. `hinge_pose` changes the pivot transform; `directional_material` creates a new material. Other functions query the evaluated scene. Update the view layer after scene or camera changes before projection. `capabilities.json` is the index.\n26\t\n27\t## Capability Name: Joint camera/layout landmark fitting\n28\t**Applicable Conditions:** Visible room corners and object landmarks, especially wide-angle interiors. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Framing, lens, positions, sizes and rotations interact. Image coordinates constrain projection but not unique depth. Observe front/back ordering and semantic face directions, not just point coordinates. **Executable Method:** `landmarks(scene,camera,points)` projects world-space points into normalized image coordinates, bottom-up; `landmark_error(projected,targets)` scores manually annotated targets `(x,y,weight)`. Start with room corners and major object endpoints, retain candidate cameras and layouts, adjust a small group at a time. Use occlusion and silhouette separately; error is not a complete image metric. **Verification Method:** render matched resolution and compare visible corners, overlap, negative space, object orientation, and crop; inspect an overhead view and multi-landmark changes. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** points outside frame, behind camera, hidden or wrongly paired can mislead; this function does not test occlusion. Joint fitting is non-unique. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** general projection knowledge; development camera transforms queried directly; historical camera adjustments are not evidence of independent improvement.\n29\t\n30\t## Capability Name: Hinged planar assemblies and occlusion\n31\t**Applicable Conditions:** Door, lid, or other hinged foreground surface. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A missing face may be hidden by a solid wall, edge-on, outside view, or occluded by another object. A broad projected face alone does not establish a valid hinge. **Executable Method:** Build leaf, panels and handle as children of one pivot at a jamb. Leave pivot unparented; `hinge_pose(pivot, hinge_world, angle_radians)` swings its local geometry around that anchor; `hinge_error(pivot,jamb_point)` reports anchor displacement. Model a real opening in the wall, or assemble wall pieces around it. Hold camera fixed while testing swing, then test camera separately. **Verification Method:** compare camera outlines and overlap, diagnose with wall temporarily hidden, and inspect top and side views, hinge alignment, clearance, leaf-to-wall and furniture intersections. Restore visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** anchor distance alone does not prove correct hinge orientation, wall opening or collision-free sweep; near-camera clipping and camera movement alter silhouette. Do not promote a visually attractive dislocated hinge to general repair. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** six-run exploration, especially door candidates and diagnostic views; observed limitations and rejected claims are detailed outside library.\n32\t\n33\t## Capability Name: Directional material and light separation\n34\t**Applicable Conditions:** Surfaces with directional grain, veining or mottling. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color pattern and illumination/highlight can look similar. Coordinate frame and anisotropic scale determine apparent pattern direction and frequency. **Executable Method:** `directional_material(name,scale,colors,roughness,coordinate)` creates a new editable Texture Coordinate → Mapping → Noise → ramp → base color network. Assign to chosen mesh slot; tune scale, colors and mapping in local coordinates. `Generated` depends on object bounds; `Object` uses object coordinates; `UV` requires a suitable UV map. Compare with plain-color roughness-only and, when appropriate, a separately authored image texture. Do not assume this noise is marble or reproduces the reference. **Verification Method:** render equal camera/exposure/lighting variants, compare surface crop, direction, contrast, edge stretching, and nearby objects. Repeat after moving light only: highlights move, fixed albedo features remain attached to surface, though illumination affects their visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** generated coordinates stretch with aspect ratio, UV seams and side faces may distort, changes of light also alter shadows. Pattern complexity does not imply accuracy. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Blender procedural mapping knowledge and the historical paired-light comparisons; direct GT node queries demonstrate various material bindings, not a unique ideal technique.\n35\t\n36\t## Capability Name: Support and connectivity screening\n37\t**Applicable Conditions:** Objects apparently resting on a floor or supported by another part. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, near contact, intersections, and disconnected assemblies are distinct. A global lowest point or AABB overlap does not verify all wheels or local supports. **Executable Method:** call `support_samples(object,support,tolerance,max_distance,sample_limit)` per explicitly selected support piece on evaluated mesh and surface. It samples lowest-band vertices and casts downward from slightly above; states are positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration or unknown support. Use `bounds_separation(a,b)` only as a broad-phase screen for potential connections, never as a contact test. Compare alternate whole-assembly translation versus localized geometry revisions, preserving pivots and seat clearance. **Verification Method:** inspect each intended contact in side views, cast or inspect additional relevant points, check for penetrations and separately inspect wheel–fork, fork–spoke, post–seat and other connections; rerun all tests after a repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** vertex sampling misses face interiors and non-lowest contacts, cannot reliably detect deep penetration, thin surfaces, overhangs, sparse meshes or unsupported regions. Ray hits are not force stability. Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of physical contact or assembly connectivity. Changing object heights can worsen silhouette and proportions. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** observed historical support tests and comparative chair edits; screening and uncertainty bounds are general technical knowledge.\n38\t\n39\t\n40\tA frozen general capability library is available read-only at /capabilities. Its entry point is /capabilities/SKILL.md. You may inspect and use its documented methods when applicable, or choose your own methods. It contains no task-specific teacher feedback or reference scene assets. Keep any dependency needed to reproduce your final source with the delivered workspace.\n41\t', 'is_error': None}], 'uuid': '6a03c92a-7c42-4041-bd59-c08cda2be382', 'parent_tool_use_id': None, 'tool_use_result': {'type': 'text', 'file': {'filePath': '/evidence/student_initial_prompt.txt', 'content': 'Given the reference image, reconstruct the depicted scene as an editable 3D scene in Blender.\n\nYou may write and execute Blender Python code, inspect the generated scene, render images, and revise your implementation.\n\nYour objective is to reconstruct the reference scene as faithfully as possible, including visible objects, geometry, spatial relationships, materials, lighting, and camera viewpoint.\n\nChoose your own reconstruction strategy and workflow.\n\nUse the provided reference image and the available Blender environment. Do not retrieve an existing reconstruction or access external reference scene data.\n\nDeliver:\n\n- Executable Blender Python source code.\n- The reconstructed .blend scene.\n- A final rendered image.\n\nWorkspace and resources:\nYour writable task workspace is /app. The reference image is also available at /app/reference.png. Blender 5.2.2 is available as blender. Rendering uses CPU only with eight threads. The student time budget is 25 minutes, with at most 120 model requests. Save the executable source entry point as /app/build.py, the editable final scene as /app/scene.blend, and the final rendered image as /app/final.png at 640 x 360 pixels. Keep any other required local source or material resources in /app so the delivered source can reproduce the scene.\n\nFrozen general capability knowledge:\n# Editable reconstruction capability library\n\nUse a single RGB as a set of constraints, not a ground-truth 3-D blueprint. Hidden geometry, absolute scale, light/material decomposition and camera/layout are underdetermined. Block the room and large objects first; use a consistent arbitrary scale and distinguish observation from hypotheses. Inspect major silhouettes, semantic facing, occlusion, vanishing directions, aspect ratios and object gaps before decorative details. Iterate camera and layout together: a plausible camera can make an incorrect layout look right, and vice versa. Preserve variants and compare fixed-camera renders before changing the view. No independent transfer improvement has been established.\n\nThe Python module is imported inside Blender (add this directory to `sys.path`). No function renders. `hinge_pose` changes the pivot transform; `directional_material` creates a new material. Other functions query the evaluated scene. Update the view layer after scene or camera changes before projection. `capabilities.json` is the index.\n\n## Capability Name: Joint camera/layout landmark fitting\n**Applicable Conditions:** Visible room corners and object landmarks, especially wide-angle interiors. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Framing, lens, positions, sizes and rotations interact. Image coordinates constrain projection but not unique depth. Observe front/back ordering and semantic face directions, not just point coordinates. **Executable Method:** `landmarks(scene,camera,points)` projects world-space points into normalized image coordinates, bottom-up; `landmark_error(projected,targets)` scores manually annotated targets `(x,y,weight)`. Start with room corners and major object endpoints, retain candidate cameras and layouts, adjust a small group at a time. Use occlusion and silhouette separately; error is not a complete image metric. **Verification Method:** render matched resolution and compare visible corners, overlap, negative space, object orientation, and crop; inspect an overhead view and multi-landmark changes. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** points outside frame, behind camera, hidden or wrongly paired can mislead; this function does not test occlusion. Joint fitting is non-unique. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** general projection knowledge; development camera transforms queried directly; historical camera adjustments are not evidence of independent improvement.\n\n## Capability Name: Hinged planar assemblies and occlusion\n**Applicable Conditions:** Door, lid, or other hinged foreground surface. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** A missing face may be hidden by a solid wall, edge-on, outside view, or occluded by another object. A broad projected face alone does not establish a valid hinge. **Executable Method:** Build leaf, panels and handle as children of one pivot at a jamb. Leave pivot unparented; `hinge_pose(pivot, hinge_world, angle_radians)` swings its local geometry around that anchor; `hinge_error(pivot,jamb_point)` reports anchor displacement. Model a real opening in the wall, or assemble wall pieces around it. Hold camera fixed while testing swing, then test camera separately. **Verification Method:** compare camera outlines and overlap, diagnose with wall temporarily hidden, and inspect top and side views, hinge alignment, clearance, leaf-to-wall and furniture intersections. Restore visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** anchor distance alone does not prove correct hinge orientation, wall opening or collision-free sweep; near-camera clipping and camera movement alter silhouette. Do not promote a visually attractive dislocated hinge to general repair. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** six-run exploration, especially door candidates and diagnostic views; observed limitations and rejected claims are detailed outside library.\n\n## Capability Name: Directional material and light separation\n**Applicable Conditions:** Surfaces with directional grain, veining or mottling. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Surface color pattern and illumination/highlight can look similar. Coordinate frame and anisotropic scale determine apparent pattern direction and frequency. **Executable Method:** `directional_material(name,scale,colors,roughness,coordinate)` creates a new editable Texture Coordinate → Mapping → Noise → ramp → base color network. Assign to chosen mesh slot; tune scale, colors and mapping in local coordinates. `Generated` depends on object bounds; `Object` uses object coordinates; `UV` requires a suitable UV map. Compare with plain-color roughness-only and, when appropriate, a separately authored image texture. Do not assume this noise is marble or reproduces the reference. **Verification Method:** render equal camera/exposure/lighting variants, compare surface crop, direction, contrast, edge stretching, and nearby objects. Repeat after moving light only: highlights move, fixed albedo features remain attached to surface, though illumination affects their visibility. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** generated coordinates stretch with aspect ratio, UV seams and side faces may distort, changes of light also alter shadows. Pattern complexity does not imply accuracy. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** Blender procedural mapping knowledge and the historical paired-light comparisons; direct GT node queries demonstrate various material bindings, not a unique ideal technique.\n\n## Capability Name: Support and connectivity screening\n**Applicable Conditions:** Objects apparently resting on a floor or supported by another part. **Problem and Technical Hypotheses:** Floating, near contact, intersections, and disconnected assemblies are distinct. A global lowest point or AABB overlap does not verify all wheels or local supports. **Executable Method:** call `support_samples(object,support,tolerance,max_distance,sample_limit)` per explicitly selected support piece on evaluated mesh and surface. It samples lowest-band vertices and casts downward from slightly above; states are positive gap, near-zero, possible penetration or unknown support. Use `bounds_separation(a,b)` only as a broad-phase screen for potential connections, never as a contact test. Compare alternate whole-assembly translation versus localized geometry revisions, preserving pivots and seat clearance. **Verification Method:** inspect each intended contact in side views, cast or inspect additional relevant points, check for penetrations and separately inspect wheel–fork, fork–spoke, post–seat and other connections; rerun all tests after a repair. **Failure Conditions and Side Effects:** vertex sampling misses face interiors and non-lowest contacts, cannot reliably detect deep penetration, thin surfaces, overhangs, sparse meshes or unsupported regions. Ray hits are not force stability. Near-zero or AABB overlap is not proof of physical contact or assembly connectivity. Changing object heights can worsen silhouette and proportions. **Evidence and Knowledge Origin:** observed historical support tests and comparative chair edits; screening and uncertainty bounds are general technical knowledge.\n\n\nA frozen general capability library is available read-only at /capabilities. Its entry point is /capabilities/SKILL.md. You may inspect and use its documented methods when applicable, or choose your own methods. It contains no task-specific teacher feedback or reference scene assets. Keep any dependency needed to reproduce your final source with the delivered workspace.\n', 'numLines': 41, 'startLine': 1, 'totalLines': 41}}, 'origin': None}
